Storage has two layers. A **store** keeps raw documents and is deliberately dumb. `createContent` wraps any store and applies every rule.

```ts
import { createContent } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { createJsonFileStore } from '@mortar-kit/core/server';

const content = createContent({ store: createJsonFileStore({ dir: 'content' }), collections, bricks });
```

## The rules

`createContent` returns a `Content`, with `list`, `get`, `save`, `remove` and `move`. Whatever the store, it:

- **normalizes on read**, so pages always get documents of the right shape;
- **validates on save**, and returns problems (`{ ok: false, issues }`) instead of throwing, so the admin can show them;
- keeps **slugs unique** in each collection;
- **moves the document on a rename** and keeps its id;
- supports `where`, `sort` and `limit` when listing;
- keeps **orderable collections in their manual order**: lists follow it, new documents go to the end, and `move` reorders (see [Manual order](/docs/collections#manual-order));
- **refuses to delete** a document while others reference it, and returns those documents;
- runs writes through one `createContent` **one at a time**, so those checks still hold when the write lands.

Collection names are checked on every call, since a server function receives them from the browser.

## The stores Mortar ships

| Store | Where | Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `createJsonFileStore({ dir })` | `@mortar-kit/core/server` | One pretty-printed file per document, `<dir>/<collection>/<slug>.json`. Content can live in git. Writes are atomic. |
| `createMemoryStore(seed?)` | `@mortar-kit/core` | In memory: tests and demos. |

The JSON-file store reads the whole folder to list a collection, which is fine for small sites.

## Calling it from the browser

`createContentServer({ content, auth })` (from `@mortar-kit/core/server`) turns a `Content` into the functions your app's `'use server'` module exports. Reads are open to everyone, since your pages render from them. Every write (saving, deleting, reordering, uploads, asset details and folders) first calls `auth.require` with the request, because middleware can't tell the admin's server-function calls apart, and answers without revalidating, so a save never refetches into the open editor. Upload limits are set once: it raises the server-function body limit to fit `createContent`'s `maxAssetSize`.

```ts
// src/server/mortar.ts (also imported by serverFunctions.serverSetup)
import { createContent } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { createContentServer, createJsonFileStore } from '@mortar-kit/core/server';

import { auth } from './auth.ts';

export const content = createContent({ store: createJsonFileStore({ dir: 'content' }), blobs, collections, bricks });
export const server = createContentServer({ content, auth });
```

```ts
// src/server/content.ts
'use server';
import { server } from './mortar.ts';

export const list = server.list;
export const get = server.get;
export const save = server.save;
export const remove = server.remove;
export const move = server.move;
export const listAssets = server.listAssets;
export const uploadAsset = server.uploadAsset;
export const removeAsset = server.removeAsset;
export const updateAsset = server.updateAsset;
export const listAssetFolders = server.listAssetFolders;
export const saveAssetFolder = server.saveAssetFolder;
export const removeAssetFolder = server.removeAssetFolder;
```

Import `src/server/mortar.ts` from the module you give the plugin as `serverFunctions.serverSetup`, so the body limit is raised before the first upload arrives.

In the browser, `contentClient(server, options)` (from `@mortar-kit/core`) builds what the rest of the app needs from those functions: `source`, cached reads for routes, layouts and bricks; `freshSource`, uncached reads for the admin's preview; and `content`, the admin's reads and writes, which drops cached reads after every write that went through. Mortar depends on neither the router nor the server-function runtime, so pass their helpers:

```ts
// src/content.ts
import { contentClient } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { query } from '@solidjs/router';
import { GET } from '@solidjs/web/server-functions';

import * as server from './server/content.ts';

export const { source, freshSource, content } = contentClient(server, { read: GET, cache: query, onWrite: () => query.clear() });
```

`read: GET` sends reads as GET requests (a POST counts as a mutation and would revalidate every query), and `cache: query` fetches a document once per request during server rendering and reuses it on hydration.
